From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stage C <stage-c@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:40:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] Inline tiny leaf functions before compiling them

Rust's MIR inliner runs before monomorphization, so a call that only becomes
concrete when a generic function is instantiated stays a call. Cranelift has no
inliner of its own, so cg_clif emits a wasm `call` for every `Shl`, `BitAnd`,
`CastInto` or `wrapping_*` helper that libm's generic maths expands to. LLVM
inlines the same helpers in its already-monomorphized IR, which is why a
software `sqrt` is several times faster there.

Add `inline_small_leaf_functions`, built on Cranelift's `Context::inline` hook,
with a deliberately narrow policy: a direct call is replaced by its body only
when the callee is a leaf of at most 12 instructions and 4 blocks, with no
stack slots, calls, memory, global or table accesses, traps or conditional
branches. A `nop`, a straight-line `jump` and the final `return` are allowed
(cg_clif gives even a one-expression wrapper an entry block that jumps to the
body). The call site still passes the same arguments, so `#[track_caller]`
locations and integer division traps behave exactly as before. The frontend
supplies the candidate bodies: only the compilation unit knows which
definitions are available, and a callee that is compiled later in the unit can
still be inlined. On A.2 (e) this takes 86.45 to 68.99 ns/point (1.25x);
stage-c/notes/codegen.md has the full result.

The test in tests/inline.rs fails before the change (the call remains) and
passes after it, and the inlined caller produces the same result under
Wasmtime. A body with a load or more than the instruction limit is rejected.
---
diff --git a/src/inline.rs b/src/inline.rs
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/inline.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,166 @@
+//! Inlining of tiny leaf functions.
+//!
+//! Rust's MIR inliner runs before monomorphization. A call that only becomes
+//! concrete when a generic function is instantiated therefore stays a call:
+//! the `<<T as Float>::Int as Shl<u32>>::shl` and `CastInto::cast` helpers that
+//! libm's generic maths expands to are the common case. LLVM inlines them in
+//! its own IR, which is already monomorphized; Cranelift has no inliner of its
+//! own, so with cg_clif each one reaches this backend as a wasm `call`.
+//! In a hot path that is expensive: the call, its argument moves and the
+//! callee's prologue cost more than the one or two arithmetic instructions the
+//! callee performs.
+//!
+//! [`inline_small_leaf_functions`] removes the smallest and most numerous of
+//! those calls before [`compile_function`](crate::compile_function) runs. The
+//! policy is deliberately narrow: only direct calls to leaf functions of at most a few blocks
+//! of at most [`MAX_INLINED_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions that perform no memory,
+//! global, table or call operations, so inlining cannot change when a trap or
+//! a side effect is observed. A frontend supplies the candidate bodies because
+//! a compilation unit, not this crate, knows which definitions are available
+//! and safe to duplicate.
+
+use crate::env::Name;
+use crate::{Error, Result};
+use alloc::borrow::Cow;
+use cranelift_codegen::Context;
+use cranelift_codegen::inline::{Inline, InlineCommand};
+use cranelift_codegen::ir::{Function, Inst, Opcode, UserFuncName, Value};
+
+/// The largest function body, in CLIF instructions, that
+/// [`inline_small_leaf_functions`] will inline.
+///
+/// The callee is copied at every call site, so this bounds both code growth
+/// and compile time. It is large enough for the one- and two-instruction
+/// wrappers that `core`'s operator impls expand to.
+pub const MAX_INLINED_INSTRUCTIONS: usize = 12;
+
+/// The largest number of CLIF blocks that an inlined body may have.
+///
+/// A cg_clif function has an entry block that jumps to the block holding the
+/// body, so even a one-expression wrapper has two. More blocks than this means
+/// real control flow, which is not worth copying into every caller.
+pub const MAX_INLINED_BLOCKS: usize = 4;
+
+/// Whether `func` is a leaf small enough for
+/// [`inline_small_leaf_functions`] to inline at each direct call.
+///
+/// A candidate has at most [`MAX_INLINED_BLOCKS`] blocks, no stack slots and
+/// at most [`MAX_INLINED_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions, each of which is a pure
+/// arithmetic, comparison, conversion or constant operation, a `nop`, an
+/// unconditional `jump`, or the final `return`. Calls, memory and global
+/// accesses, traps and conditional branches are excluded, so duplicating the
+/// body cannot duplicate or reorder an observable effect.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn is_small_leaf(func: &Function) -> bool {
+    func.dfg.num_blocks() <= MAX_INLINED_BLOCKS
+        && func.stencil.sized_stack_slots.is_empty()
+        && func.dfg.num_insts() <= MAX_INLINED_INSTRUCTIONS
+        && func.signature.params.len() <= 4
+        && func.signature.returns.len() <= 1
+        && func.layout.blocks().all(|block| {
+            func.layout
+                .block_insts(block)
+                .all(|inst| is_pure(func, inst))
+        })
+}
+
+/// Is `inst` an operation that can be copied to another function without
+/// carrying memory, a global, a call or a trap with it?
+///
+/// Matching on the instruction *format* rather than an opcode list is what
+/// makes this robust: every instruction that reads or writes memory, names a
+/// global, references a table entry or calls a function has its own format,
+/// so none of them can match. `return` is the only multi-argument operation
+/// allowed; the other forms are integer and floating-point arithmetic.
+fn is_pure(func: &Function, inst: Inst) -> bool {
+    use cranelift_codegen::ir::InstructionData as D;
+    match &func.dfg.insts[inst] {
+        D::Binary { .. }
+        | D::BinaryImm8 { .. }
+        | D::FloatCompare { .. }
+        | D::IntCompare { .. }
+        | D::Ternary { .. }
+        | D::TernaryImm8 { .. }
+        | D::Unary { .. }
+        | D::UnaryImm { .. }
+        | D::UnaryIeee16 { .. }
+        | D::UnaryIeee32 { .. }
+        | D::UnaryIeee64 { .. } => true,
+        // The entry block that cg_clif adds jumps to the body block. A jump is
+        // control flow, but it has no effect of its own; conditional branches
+        // and traps are still rejected, so a body is at most a straight line.
+        D::Jump { .. } => true,
+        D::NullAry { opcode } => matches!(opcode, Opcode::Nop | Opcode::SequencePoint),
+        D::MultiAry { opcode, .. } => *opcode == Opcode::Return,
+        _ => false,
+    }
+}
+
+/// Inline direct calls in `func` to the small leaf functions in `callees`.
+///
+/// A callee is matched by [`Function::name`]; a frontend that reuses this must
+/// set it to the name its calls resolve to. Only the caller is changed: every
+/// callee is still compiled and emitted on its own, so an inlined body is a
+/// copy, not a move.
+///
+/// Returns whether at least one call was inlined.
+///
+/// # Errors
+///
+/// Returns [`Error::Unsupported`] if Cranelift's inliner rejects the function.
+/// That indicates invalid IR, which [`compile_function`](crate::compile_function)
+/// would reject too.
+pub fn inline_small_leaf_functions(func: &mut Function, callees: &[Function]) -> Result<bool> {
+    let mut context = Context::new();
+    context.func = core::mem::replace(func, Function::new());
+    let inlined = context
+        .inline(SmallLeafInliner { callees })
+        .map_err(|e| Error::Unsupported(alloc::format!("inlining: {e}")))?;
+    *func = context.func;
+    Ok(inlined)
+}
+
+/// The [`Inline`] policy used by [`inline_small_leaf_functions`].
+struct SmallLeafInliner<'a> {
+    callees: &'a [Function],
+}
+
+impl Inline for SmallLeafInliner<'_> {
+    fn inline(
+        &mut self,
+        caller: &Function,
+        _call_inst: Inst,
+        call_opcode: Opcode,
+        callee: cranelift_codegen::ir::FuncRef,
+        _call_args: &[Value],
+    ) -> InlineCommand<'_> {
+        if call_opcode != Opcode::Call {
+            return InlineCommand::KeepCall;
+        }
+        let name = Name::resolve(caller, &caller.dfg.ext_funcs[callee].name);
+        let callees = self.callees;
+        let Some(body) = callees
+            .iter()
+            .find(|body| has_name(body, name) && is_small_leaf(body))
+        else {
+            return InlineCommand::KeepCall;
+        };
+        InlineCommand::Inline {
+            callee: Cow::Borrowed(body),
+            // The body is a leaf, so it cannot contain another call to
+            // consider. Not revisiting it also bounds the pass at one level.
+            visit_callee: false,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Whether a call to `name` resolves to `body`.
+fn has_name(body: &Function, name: Name<'_>) -> bool {
+    match (name, &body.name) {
+        (Name::User(user), UserFuncName::User(body)) => user == body,
+        (Name::TestCase(testcase), UserFuncName::Testcase(body)) => {
+            testcase.as_bytes() == body.raw()
+        }
+        _ => false,
+    }
+}
